README.md-16-
README.md-17-Minimal authenticated Streamable HTTP MCP for the local Windows shell/process control boundary. The public endpoint remains stable while replaceable loopback backends are built, tested, switched, and drained behind a transparent front door.
README.md-18-
README.md:19:The default full worker-visible contract is exactly `view_image`, `start_process`, `read_output`, `kill_process`, `busy_list`, `busy_claim`, and `busy_release`; the opt-in `process` profile intentionally exposes only the three process tools.
README.md:20:## Minimal redundant connector profile
README.md-21-
README.md:22:For regression-research #125, `MCP_TOOL_PROFILE=process` exposes only `start_process`, `read_output`, and `kill_process`. The default `full` profile remains unchanged. Minimal connector instances are stateless per MCP request, keep their OAuth/transport state separate, and may share the process receipt directory. Completed process receipts are readable through any clone; while a process is still running, cross-clone `read_output` and `kill_process` are relayed through a shared local control mailbox to the clone that actually owns the in-memory process. No backup clone kills a PID directly. BUSY/job coordination stays outside MCP in the standalone Rust/Python tool apps and is not duplicated into the connector.
README.md-23-
README.md:24:Use `scripts/start-minimal-clone.ps1` for one instance or `scripts/start-two-minimal-clones.ps1` with `config/minimal-clones.example.json` for the initial two-instance rollout. The sample config is intentionally only two instances; scale-out remains configuration, not code copies. `MCP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` may include a path prefix (for example `/clone-a`) so multiple independently registered connectors can share ordinary HTTPS 443 while advertising distinct OAuth/resource identities.
README.md-25-
README.md-26-The stable front door can also terminate those clone paths before forwarding them to loopback-only clone listeners. Put a version-1 route map such as `{ "version": 1, "routes": { "clone-a": [3021, 3011], "clone-b": [3022, 3012] } }` at `.state/front-door/static-routes.json`. Ports are tried in order, while a single scalar port remains accepted for compatibility. Requests under `/clone-a/*` and `/clone-b/*` are forwarded with that public prefix stripped, while the path-scoped OAuth/OpenID `.well-known` endpoints are forwarded unchanged. This lets Tailscale Funnel use one local target (`127.0.0.1:3003`) instead of exposing each clone listener as a separate Funnel handler. The normal root backend generation/pinning path is unchanged.
README.md-27-
README.md-28-### Root ingress recovery
README.md-29-
README.md:30:Static clone routing must not disable reconciliation of the public root handler. Keep a single public Funnel handler at `/` targeting `http://127.0.0.1:3003`. Do **not** create separate Tailscale `--set-path /clone-a` or `/clone-b` handlers while 3003 static clone routing is enabled: Tailscale strips that path prefix before forwarding, so the front door sees clone OAuth/MCP requests as root traffic and routes them to the root backend. Clone paths must instead fall through the root Funnel handler with `/clone-a/*` or `/clone-b/*` intact so 3003 can select the configured static clone backend. Verify both `tailscale funnel status` and an authenticated end-to-end clone smoke test; a configured handler or `/health` response alone does not prove connector continuity. During the 2026-09-01 plugin2 incident, commit `39f205a`'s early return left the root handler stale; removing that guard and re-pointing the root handler restored public `/health`. The user-reported plugin2 recovery remains separately labeled in the incident record.
README.md-31-
README.md-32-
README.md:33:[2026-09-01 production quarantine] `/clone-a` is pinned to the already-running prefix-safe generation on port `3041`. It reuses the stable `clone-a` OAuth store and shared process-receipt directory. Port `3051` remains alive only as rollback evidence and MUST NOT be restored to `/clone-a` without new end-to-end connector proof; ports `3011/3012` also remain non-production fallback/test generations. Keep the public identity unchanged and change only the local Funnel target when recovering this connector.
--
